Liuzijue (六字訣), the "six-character formula" or "Six Healing Sounds", is [qigong](./qigong.md) built on the exhalation. Six sounds are breathed out slowly, sometimes silently, and each is paired with an organ-function system of [zang-fu](./zang-fu.md) theory:
- **xū (噓):** the liver;
- **hē (呵):** the heart;
- **hū (呼):** the spleen;
- **sī (呬):** the lung;
- **chuī (吹):** the kidney;
- **xī (嘻):** the [san jiao](./san-jiao.md).

Its earliest known description is in the *Yangxing yanming lu* ("Record of Nourishing Nature and Extending Life"), associated with the fifth-to-sixth-century Daoist master Tao Hongjing. There, the six exhalations expel illness and are drawn in against one intake breath [@kohn2008].

Of the four standardized forms of 2003, Liuzijue is the one where regulating the breath, the second of the [three regulations](./san-tiao.md), carries most of the practice. Each sound shapes a long, controlled exhalation, and gentle movements accompany it. In physiological terms it is a structured form of [slow-paced breathing](../../../../../../../physiology-services-section/content/text/glossary/terms/slow-paced-breathing.md) with prolonged exhalation. That is one reason it has been trialled in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.

Pattern diagnosis (辨證, biàn zhèng, "differentiating the pattern") is how [traditional Chinese medicine](./traditional-chinese-medicine.md) reasons from a patient to a treatment. The practitioner gathers signs through the four examinations: looking (including the tongue), listening and smelling, asking, and touching (above all the pulse). The practitioner then reads them together as one *pattern* (證, zhèng) of disharmony: a configuration of excess or deficiency, heat or cold, and of the [substances](./qi.md) and [organ-function systems](./zang-fu.md) involved [@kaptchuk2000].

The pattern, not the named disease, decides the treatment. This principle is called "treatment follows pattern differentiation" (biàn zhèng lùn zhì). Two consequences follow, and clinical teaching states them as maxims: one disease may be treated differently in different patients (tóng bìng yì zhì), and different diseases may be treated alike when they present the same pattern (yì bìng tóng zhì).

Several frames organize the reading, each suited to different conditions [@maciocia2015]:

- the [eight principles](./eight-principles.md), the first and most general: interior or exterior, cold or heat, deficiency or excess, yin or yang;
- the organ-function systems ([zang-fu](./zang-fu.md)) and the substances: [qi](./qi.md), [blood](./xue.md) and [fluids](./jin-ye.md);
- the six stages of the *Shānghán lùn*, for disorders from external cold;
- the four levels of defensive, qi, nutritive and blood, from the warm-disease school, for febrile disorders;
- the [san jiao](./san-jiao.md).

The practice is explained at length in the [pattern diagnosis note](../../documents/articles/notes/pattern-diagnosis.md) and applied in [common TCM patterns](../../documents/articles/notes/common-tcm-patterns.md).

The Spleen (脾, pí) is one of the five yin organ-function systems of [zang-fu](./zang-fu.md) theory. It names a set of functions, not the anatomical spleen. In biomedical terms those functions sit closer to digestion and absorption as a whole than to any single organ.

Its central function is transformation and transportation (yùn huà). The Spleen extracts the refined essence of food and drink, sends it upward to the Lung and Heart, and so provides the material from which [qi](./qi.md) and [blood](./xue.md) are made. It is called the root of postnatal life, the source that replenishes what is spent after birth, as [jing](./jing.md) is the root of what was received before it [@maciocia2015].

The tradition assigns it further functions:
- it governs the blood, keeping it within the vessels (bleeding and bruising easily are read as Spleen qi failing to hold);
- it raises the clear qi, which holds the organs in place (prolapse is read as its failure);
- it controls the muscles and the four limbs;
- it opens into the mouth and shows in the lips.

It belongs to the earth phase of [wu xing](./wu-xing.md), is paired with the Stomach, and is said to dislike dampness: weak Spleen function lets fluids stagnate into damp and [phlegm](./phlegm.md). Tiredness after eating, loose stools, a poor appetite and heavy limbs are the classic signs of Spleen qi deficiency, one of the commonest patterns in [pattern diagnosis](./pattern-diagnosis.md).

Traditional Chinese Medicine (中醫, zhōngyī, "Chinese medicine") is the system of diagnosis and treatment developed in China from the classical canons: the *Huangdi Neijing* (in two parts, *Suwen* and *Lingshu*), the *Nanjing*, and the *Shanghan lun*. It was elaborated by two millennia of physicians and schools.

The physiology it reasons in is functional. The body is described through:

- the substances of [qi](./qi.md), [blood](./xue.md), [body fluids](./jin-ye.md), and [essence](./jing.md);
- the organ-function systems of [zang-fu](./zang-fu.md);
- the [channels](./meridians.md) that connect them;
- the organizing frames of [yin-yang](./yin-yang.md) and the [five phases](./wu-xing.md).

Clinical reasoning identifies a pattern (證, zhèng), for which the [eight principles](./eight-principles.md) are one frame. It treats the pattern through acupuncture and moxibustion, herbal prescription, tuina massage, dietetics, and [qigong](./qigong.md) [@kaptchuk2000].

"TCM" in the narrow sense names the standardized, institutional form of this medicine created in the People's Republic of China from the 1950s. It was systematized into textbooks and colleges and integrated with biomedicine in state hospitals [@scheid2002]. The older, plural practice it drew from is often called Chinese medicine or classical Chinese medicine. This section uses "traditional Chinese medicine" for the system as a whole and marks the distinction where it matters [@unschuld1985].

This section studies the system in its own terms, as the [section README](../../../../README.md) states. Its concepts are recorded as a coherent carving of the body with its own criteria of success. Biomedical findings are recorded as external commentary on it, not as its adjudication.

Wuqinxi (五禽戲), the "Five Animal Frolics", is a moving [qigong](./qigong.md) sequence that imitates five animals: the tiger, deer, bear, monkey, and bird (usually the crane).

The Han-dynasty histories attribute it to Hua Tuo, the second-century physician. They quote him saying that the body needs movement as a door-hinge does, which does not rot because it turns, and that the frolics were devised to move it. It belongs to the older tradition of animal postures in [daoyin](./daoyin.md): the "bear-hangings and bird-stretches" the *Zhuangzi* already names [@kohn2008].

Each animal pairs a manner of movement with an intended effect in the practice's own account:
- **the tiger:** power, with clawing and pouncing;
- **the deer:** stretching and turning the spine;
- **the bear:** heavy, rooted swaying that massages the abdomen;
- **the monkey:** light, quick agility;
- **the bird:** balance on one leg with outspread arms.

The Chinese Health Qigong Association standardized a ten-movement version, two per animal, in 2003. Its playfulness and variety make it the most expressive of the four standard forms. As with all of them, the imitation is carried by the [three regulations](./san-tiao.md) of body, breath, and mind.
